def T(a, y, m, s, w, q):
    return dict(a=a, y=y, m=m, s=s, w=w, q=q)

C = {
"Federal government building construction cleaning": T(
 "Federal government building construction cleaning is the rough, final and touch-up cleaning of new or renovated federal office buildings, finished to the agency's closeout checklist and the prime contractor's site safety plan before acceptance.",
 "Federal acceptance walks are formal and documented. Dust, labels or debris left behind can hold up substantial completion, delay occupancy and add punch-list rounds the prime contractor has to manage.",
 "Our crews work under the prime contractor's site safety plan, clean floor by floor with HEPA methods and log each area against the agency's closeout checklist before the government walk.",
 ["Gross square footage and floor count", "Phase: rough, final or touch-up", "Agency closeout checklist and acceptance walk dates", "Badging and background checks as the facility requires", "Wage determination and certified payroll where required"],
 ["Badging and background checks can take weeks, so start them early through the prime contractor.",
  "Wage determinations in the contract apply to cleaning labor and must be priced in.",
  "Secure or restricted rooms may need escorts, which slows production rates."],
 [("What does a final clean on a federal building include?", "Floors, glass, restrooms, fixtures, millwork, vents, doors and hardware, all cleaned and checked against the agency's closeout checklist so the acceptance walk focuses on construction items, not dust."),
  ("Do cleaning crews on federal projects need background checks?", "Often, yes. Background checks and badging are handled as the facility requires and are arranged through the prime contractor, so we submit crew names well before the start date."),
  ("Does Davis-Bacon apply to construction cleaning on a federal job?", "It can. When the contract carries Davis-Bacon or Service Contract Act wage determinations, we pay and report cleaning labor as the contract requires, including certified payroll where required."),
  ("How far ahead should we schedule cleaning for a federal turnover?", "Plan three to six weeks out for a large building, since access paperwork, phased floor releases and the acceptance walk date all drive the crew schedule."),
  ("Can your crews follow the prime contractor's safety plan?", "Yes. Our crews attend site orientation, follow the prime contractor's site safety plan and activity hazard analyses, and wear the protective equipment the plan calls for."),
  ("Who sets the standard for clean on a government project?", "The contract and the agency's closeout requirements set it. We clean to that checklist and to the prime contractor's punch list, and document completed areas for the record.")]),

"State and municipal government facility cleaning": T(
 "State and municipal government facility cleaning covers construction cleanup and final cleaning of city halls, county buildings, state offices and public service centers, prepared for the public agency's inspection and move-in.",
 "Public buildings open to residents on fixed dates, often with ribbon cuttings and council tours. A clean, safe lobby and service counter matter as much as the finished construction.",
 "Our crews phase the clean around public meeting dates, finish lobbies and counters first, and follow the general contractor's safety plan and any local wage rules the contract includes.",
 ["Building size and number of public-facing areas", "Council chambers, service counters and records rooms", "Opening or ribbon-cutting date", "Prevailing wage or local wage rules as the contract requires", "Exterior walks, plazas and entries"],
 ["Records rooms and evidence rooms may be locked or restricted during cleaning.",
  "Public opening dates rarely move, so build in a touch-up day.",
  "State and local prevailing wage rules vary and must be checked per contract."],
 [("What is included in a city hall construction clean?", "Lobbies, service counters, council chambers, offices, restrooms, glass and exterior entries are cleaned, with extra attention to public areas the community will see on opening day."),
  ("Do municipal cleaning jobs require prevailing wage?", "Some do. Many state and local public works contracts include prevailing wage rules, and we price and pay cleaning labor as the contract requires, with certified payroll where required."),
  ("Can you clean a county building before a ribbon cutting?", "Yes. We schedule the final clean to finish before the ceremony and hold a crew for a same-day touch-up of entries, glass and restrooms if trades are still finishing."),
  ("How do you handle council chambers and dais furniture?", "Crews dust and wipe dais millwork, clean audio-visual surfaces with dry methods, vacuum seating and detail glass partitions without disturbing installed microphones or cabling."),
  ("Do you clean records rooms in new government offices?", "Yes, when the agency releases them. Shelving, floors and fixtures are HEPA vacuumed and wiped before records move in, and locked rooms are cleaned with an escort if required."),
  ("Who do we contact to schedule a municipal building clean?", "Contact us with the plans, square footage and opening date. We confirm crew availability and phasing with the general contractor or the public works project manager.")]),

"Federal courthouse and administrative building cleaning": T(
 "Federal courthouse and administrative building cleaning is the construction final clean of courtrooms, chambers, jury suites, holding areas and offices, carried out within the building's security screening and access rules.",
 "Courtrooms carry high-finish millwork, stone and glass that show every smudge, and court calendars set hard opening dates. Security procedures also add time that must be planned into the clean.",
 "Our crews pass through security screening with an approved tool and supply list, clean courtrooms top down, protect millwork finishes and coordinate secure areas with the prime contractor.",
 ["Courtrooms, chambers and jury rooms", "Holding cells and secure corridors", "Security screening and approved tool lists", "Millwork, stone and glass finishes", "Escort needs for restricted zones"],
 ["Every tool and chemical may be screened at entry, so submit lists ahead of time.",
  "Wood paneling and benches need finish-safe cleaners and soft cloths.",
  "Secure corridors and holding areas often require escorted work only."],
 [("How do cleaning crews get through courthouse security?", "Crews pass the building's security screening each day. We submit names, tool lists and chemical lists in advance through the prime contractor so equipment is cleared quickly."),
  ("What areas of a courthouse need special cleaning care?", "Courtroom millwork, judges' benches, stone floors, jury box seating and glass partitions need finish-safe products and careful detailing, since they are the most visible surfaces."),
  ("Are holding cells cleaned during courthouse construction?", "Yes, as part of the final clean, with escort where the facility requires. Crews clean detention fixtures, floors and walls and account for every tool before leaving the area."),
  ("When should the courthouse final clean be scheduled?", "After furniture and audio-visual installation and before the agency walk. Courtrooms often get a second touch-up right before the first scheduled hearing."),
  ("Can you clean an administrative building while staff move in?", "Yes. We phase floors ahead of the move schedule and keep a small crew on hand to clean up after furniture installers and movers."),
  ("Do courthouse projects need background checks for cleaning staff?", "Usually. Background checks and badging are completed as the facility requires and are arranged through the prime contractor before crews arrive on site.")]),

"Police, fire and emergency services facility cleaning": T(
 "Police, fire and emergency services facility cleaning is the construction final clean of police stations, fire stations and EMS stations, including apparatus bays, bunk rooms, decontamination rooms, sally ports and dispatch areas.",
 "First responders move in fast and stay around the clock. Concrete dust in bays, debris in decon rooms or dirty dispatch consoles can affect equipment and crews from the first shift.",
 "Our crews scrub apparatus bay floors, clean trench drains, detail decon and bunk rooms, and dry-clean dispatch areas, working around station staff if the building is partly occupied.",
 ["Apparatus bays and trench drains", "Bunk rooms, kitchens and fitness areas", "Decontamination and gear rooms", "Sally ports, interview rooms and evidence areas", "Dispatch and communications rooms"],
 ["Evidence and armory rooms are restricted and usually cleaned under escort.",
  "Trench drains clog with construction debris and must be cleared before apparatus move in.",
  "Dispatch consoles need dry methods only around live electronics."],
 [("What does a fire station construction clean include?", "Apparatus bay floors, trench drains, bunk rooms, kitchen, restrooms, gear and decon rooms, and offices are cleaned so the station can take apparatus and crews on move-in day."),
  ("How are apparatus bay floors cleaned after construction?", "Crews sweep, then machine scrub sealed concrete with neutral cleaner, flush debris from trench drains and dry the floor so coatings and line markings stay intact."),
  ("Can you clean a police station with officers already inside?", "Yes. We phase work around occupied areas, keep restricted rooms closed unless escorted, and stage equipment so it does not block sally ports or stairways."),
  ("Do you clean 911 dispatch rooms during construction?", "We do, using HEPA vacuums and dry microfiber around consoles and raised floors, and we coordinate with the systems installer before touching any live equipment."),
  ("Is an EMS station cleaned differently than an office?", "The decontamination room, supply rooms and bays get extra attention. Crews clean them with disinfectant protocols after construction dust is removed."),
  ("How long does a fire station final clean take?", "A typical single-company station takes two to four crew days, depending on bay size, finishes and how many trades are still finishing punch items.")]),

"Correctional and detention facility cleaning": T(
 "Correctional and detention facility cleaning is construction cleanup and final cleaning of jails, prisons and detention centers, carried out under strict tool control, contraband rules and escorted work inside secure areas.",
 "Anything left behind in a secure facility can become contraband or a weapon. Missing tools, loose screws and stray debris can delay activation, and dust in security electronics causes faults.",
 "Our crews check tools in and out on written inventories, work only under escort in secure zones, remove every piece of debris and clean security hardware with approved methods.",
 ["Housing units, cells and dayrooms", "Tool control inventory and check-in procedures", "Escort schedule and secure-zone access", "Security glazing, detention hardware and control rooms", "Kitchen, laundry and medical units"],
 ["A single missing tool can lock down the site, so inventories are checked at every exit.",
  "Personal items and phones are often banned inside the secure perimeter.",
  "Escort availability sets the pace, so production rates run lower than commercial work.",
  "Detention-grade glazing scratches if cleaned with abrasive pads."],
 [("What is tool control on a jail construction project?", "Every tool is logged in and out on a written inventory, kept within reach, and counted before crews leave a secure area, so nothing can become contraband or a weapon."),
  ("Do cleaners need an escort inside a prison?", "In secure zones, almost always. We schedule work around the escort plan set by the facility and the prime contractor, which affects daily output and pricing."),
  ("What contraband rules apply to cleaning crews in a detention center?", "Phones, lighters, glass containers and some chemicals may be banned. Crews bring only approved items, and chemicals are logged and stored where the facility allows."),
  ("How are cells cleaned after construction?", "Crews remove all debris, vacuum and wipe detention fixtures, clean security glazing with non-abrasive products and check that no screws, wire or packaging are left behind."),
  ("Are security control rooms included in the final clean?", "Yes, using HEPA vacuums and dry microfiber around consoles, and only with permission from the electronics installer when systems are live."),
  ("Why does detention facility cleaning cost more than office cleaning?", "Escorts, tool counts, screening at every entry and contraband rules all slow production, so the same square footage takes more labor hours than a commercial final clean.")]),

"Disaster response and emergency operations facility cleaning": T(
 "Disaster response and emergency operations facility cleaning covers construction and renovation cleaning of emergency operations centers, warehouses and response hubs, planned around 24/7 readiness so the facility can activate at any time.",
 "An emergency operations center may activate mid-project. Dust in servers, debris in operations rooms or blocked corridors can slow a response when the community needs it most.",
 "Our crews keep operations floors, corridors and exits clear at the end of each shift, HEPA clean around communications equipment and can stand down or speed up when the center activates.",
 ["Operations floor, breakout and briefing rooms", "Communications and server rooms", "Generator, fuel and mechanical areas", "Supply warehouse and loading areas", "Stand-down plan for activations"],
 ["Activation can halt work with little notice, so leave areas clean each shift.",
  "Communications rooms need dry, HEPA-only methods around energized equipment.",
  "Response warehouses must keep aisles and dock doors clear at all times."],
 [("Can you clean an emergency operations center that must stay ready?", "Yes. We plan the clean in zones, leave every area usable at the end of each shift and follow a stand-down plan if the center activates during work."),
  ("What happens to cleaning if the EOC activates mid-project?", "Crews stop, clear equipment from operations areas and exits, and resume when facility managers release the space, usually with a quick touch-up of shared areas."),
  ("How are communications rooms cleaned in an emergency center?", "With HEPA vacuums, dry microfiber and static-safe practices around racks and consoles, coordinated with the systems installer before any live equipment is touched."),
  ("Do you clean disaster response warehouses after construction?", "Yes. Crews sweep and scrub floors, clean racking and dock areas, and keep aisles open so supplies can be staged as soon as the building is released."),
  ("Can cleaning be done at night at an operations center?", "Often, yes. Night and weekend shifts reduce conflicts with drills and daily staff, and we price them separately when the schedule calls for them."),
  ("Is there a fast turnaround option before an emergency center opens?", "We can add crews for a compressed final clean when an opening date moves up, subject to crew availability and how quickly site access is processed.")]),

"Government building decommissioning cleanup": T(
 "Government building decommissioning cleanup is the cleaning and debris removal that prepares a vacated public facility for transfer, sale, demolition or reuse, coordinated with hazardous-material surveys and the agency's closeout requirements.",
 "Decommissioned buildings can hold abandoned furniture, records debris and regulated materials. Clean, documented spaces support property transfer, inspections and the next contract phase without surprises.",
 "Our crews clear non-regulated debris, broom clean and HEPA vacuum rooms, and document each space, while regulated materials stay with licensed technicians identified in the hazardous-material survey.",
 ["Building size and remaining contents", "Hazardous-material survey results and exclusion areas", "Debris hauling, recycling and disposal", "Broom-clean or move-out standard required", "Photo documentation for the agency"],
 ["Do not disturb suspected asbestos, lead or PCB materials before the survey is reviewed.",
  "Regulated materials are handled only by licensed technicians, never general cleaning crews.",
  "Records, media and equipment may have disposal rules set by the agency."],
 [("What is government building decommissioning cleanup?", "It is the cleanout of a vacated public building, removing debris and leftover items and cleaning the space so it can be transferred, sold, demolished or reused."),
  ("Do you remove asbestos during decommissioning?", "No. Asbestos, lead and other regulated materials are handled only by licensed technicians. Our crews work outside regulated areas identified in the hazardous-material survey."),
  ("Why is a hazardous-material survey needed before cleanup?", "The survey shows where regulated materials are so crews avoid disturbing them. It sets exclusion areas and the order of work before any general cleaning starts."),
  ("What standard of clean is used for a decommissioned building?", "Usually broom clean or the agency's own closeout standard, confirmed in the contract. We document each room with photos when the agency requires it."),
  ("Can you haul debris from a closed government building?", "Yes. We remove non-regulated debris and furniture, separate recyclables and dispose of materials through permitted facilities, following any agency rules for records or equipment."),
  ("How long does a decommissioning cleanout usually take?", "It depends on remaining contents and square footage. An emptied office building can take a few days, while one with furniture and debris may take several weeks.")]),

"Government historic preservation project cleaning": T(
 "Government historic preservation project cleaning is gentle construction cleaning of historic courthouses, capitols, post offices and landmark public buildings, using methods that protect original materials during and after restoration.",
 "Historic plaster, stone, wood and decorative metal can be damaged permanently by harsh chemicals, abrasion or water. Preservation rules on public buildings often require reviewed, documented methods.",
 "Our crews test methods on small areas first, use HEPA vacuums, soft brushes and pH-neutral cleaners, and follow the preservation specification and the architect's guidance for each material.",
 ["Historic materials and finishes in scope", "Preservation specification and approved methods", "Test panels and mockups", "Protection of original features during cleaning", "Documentation for the agency's preservation review"],
 ["Acidic or abrasive cleaners can etch marble and historic stone permanently.",
  "Excess water can stain plaster and swell historic wood floors.",
  "Older buildings may contain lead paint, so disturbance must be avoided."],
 [("How do you clean a historic courthouse after restoration?", "Crews start with HEPA vacuuming and dry methods, test any wet cleaning on a hidden area, and use pH-neutral products and soft tools approved for each historic material."),
  ("What cleaning products are safe for historic marble?", "Generally pH-neutral stone cleaners and plain water in small amounts. Acids, vinegar and abrasive powders can etch marble, so methods are confirmed with the project's preservation specification."),
  ("Why are test areas used in historic building cleaning?", "A test shows how a material reacts before the whole surface is cleaned. It lets the architect or preservation reviewer approve the method and avoid permanent damage."),
  ("Can construction dust harm historic plaster and woodwork?", "Yes. Fine dust settles into carvings and moldings and becomes harder to remove over time. Soft brushes and HEPA vacuums lift it without abrasion."),
  ("Do you clean exterior masonry on historic public buildings?", "We clean light construction residue with low-pressure, gentle methods. Heavy restoration cleaning of historic masonry follows the preservation specification and its approved test panels."),
  ("What if a historic building has lead paint?", "Crews avoid disturbing painted surfaces that may contain lead. Any lead-related work is handled by licensed technicians under the applicable rules, separate from general cleaning.")]),

"Government infrastructure construction cleanup": T(
 "Government infrastructure construction cleanup is jobsite and final cleaning on publicly funded projects such as pump stations, treatment plant buildings, maintenance yards and public works facilities, done to the agency's closeout requirements.",
 "Public works projects are inspected by the agency before acceptance. Debris, mud and dust left in control rooms or around equipment can hold up final payment and system startup.",
 "Our crews handle progress cleanup and final cleaning, keep debris out of drains and equipment, follow the prime contractor's site safety plan and track work against the closeout checklist.",
 ["Site area, buildings and equipment rooms", "Progress cleanup frequency", "Final clean ahead of agency inspection", "Drain inlet and stormwater protection", "Wage rules and certified payroll where required"],
 ["Equipment rooms may have energized gear and lockout rules to follow.",
  "Wash water and debris must be kept out of storm drains.",
  "Confined spaces such as wet wells require permits and trained entrants."],
 [("What is included in public works construction cleanup?", "Ongoing debris pickup, dust control and housekeeping during construction, then a final clean of buildings, equipment rooms and site areas before the agency's inspection."),
  ("Do you clean pump stations and treatment plant buildings?", "Yes, the buildings and accessible equipment areas. Crews follow lockout rules around equipment and do not enter permit-required confined spaces without the required permits and training."),
  ("How often should a government construction site be cleaned?", "Weekly progress cleanup is common, with added visits before inspections. Busy sites with many trades may need cleanup several times a week."),
  ("Are prevailing wages required for infrastructure cleanup work?", "On many public projects, yes. We pay cleaning labor under the wage determinations or local wage rules as the contract requires, with certified payroll where required."),
  ("How do you keep stormwater clean during site cleanup?", "Crews protect drain inlets, sweep instead of hosing debris, and collect wash water so it does not reach storm drains, following the project's stormwater plan."),
  ("Can you work on a site with active traffic or equipment?", "Yes, under the prime contractor's traffic control and site safety plan. Crews wear high-visibility gear and work only in released areas.")]),
}
